WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
We know that Delta of
f is a root of unity,

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:06.160
with residue one above
every prime dividing u.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.820
First isolate the general local argument.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.300
In a local domain with
residue characteristic p,

00:00:13.473 --> 00:00:16.260
let xi be a root of
unity with residue one.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.820
Write its order as p to the a
times m, where p does not divide m.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.400
Raise xi to p to the a,
and call the result eta.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.430
Its order is m, and its
residue is still one.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.630
Suppose m is greater than
one, so eta is not one.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.940
The geometric identity factors the
difference between eta to the m and

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.880
one as eta minus one times
the sum of its first m powers,

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.870
starting at one. Eta to the m is one.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.280
Since we are in a domain and eta minus
one is nonzero, that sum must vanish.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.760
The closed polygon illustrates this
exact sum in the complex plane.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.160
Its arrows are separate from
the local residue calculation.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.340
Now reduce the vanishing
sum in the residue field.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.300
Each of its m terms becomes one. Their
sum is the residue of the integer m,

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:16.300
which is nonzero because p does not divide
m. This contradicts the vanishing sum.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.080
Therefore m is one: the original root
has p-power order. Order one is allowed.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.920
Apply this to Delta of f, with
u odd and greater than one.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.660
For every prime p dividing u, we
have residue one at a prime above p.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.510
Such primes exist: the
cyclotomic ring is a nonzero

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.410
free abelian group of finite rank,
so its quotient by p is nonzero.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.900
The local argument makes the
order of Delta a power of p.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.320
Since every prime dividing
u is odd, the order is odd.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.060
If u has two distinct prime divisors
p and q, the order is a power of both.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:02.060
The only common positive integer is
one, so Delta equals one in that case.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.360
With only one prime divisor, a
nontrivial rotation can remain.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.480
Odd order is the conclusion
that covers both cases.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.680
For u equal to three, Delta lies in
the field generated by i and omega,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.460
of degree four over the rationals.
Its order is a power of three.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.320
A primitive ninth root has degree
six, so it cannot lie in this field.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.600
Higher powers of three
require still larger degrees.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.640
Only three directions remain:
one, omega, and omega squared.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.120
For the actual norm example
f of P equals three i P,

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.920
f of one is three i, and f
of omega is three i omega.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.060
Their quotient is omega
inverse, or omega squared.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.260
The phase therefore need not be zero.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.060
Applying the theorem to c, d, and
g gives three odd-order phases.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.840
Node six will combine that arithmetic
fact with their common sign origin.
